00_GQuickStart
==============

A short but complete Geneva program, the one the manual's Quick Start chapter
walks through. A single source file, GQuickStart.cpp, holds

  * the problem: a class FirstProblem with two floating-point parameters x and
    y in [-5, 5], whose evaluate() returns f(x, y) = (x - 1)^2 + (y + 2)^2;
  * how the evolutionary algorithm mutates them (adaptionConfig());
  * a main() that hands the problem to Go2, records the search with a progress
    plotter, runs it and prints the best solution found.

The minimum is known in advance -- f = 0 at (1, -2) -- so the result can be
checked by eye; the program also checks it itself and exits non-zero if the
search missed it.

Running it
----------

From this directory in the build tree:

  ./GQuickStart

The build has placed the configuration files with their default values in
./config/; a copy of the program elsewhere writes them on its first run. The
default algorithm is an evolutionary algorithm, and the candidates are
evaluated on all cores. Useful options:

  ./GQuickStart -a swarm      another algorithm (or a chain, e.g. -a ea,swarm)
  ./GQuickStart --help        every option

After the run, progressScan.C holds every evaluated candidate over the two
parameters; draw it with ROOT:

  root -l progressScan.C

Where to go next
----------------

01_GSimpleOptimizer runs the same kind of search on a choice of standard test
functions from the command line. For a project of your own, outside this tree,
start from ../../quickstart, which builds against an installed Geneva.
